---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { supportEmail } from '../lib/site';
---
<SiteLayout title="Privacy Policy — Omnibar for Safari" description="Omnibar collects no extension data. How its address bar and command palette handle tabs, searches, local settings, and optional Kagi links." section="privacy">
  <div class="document wrap">
    <header class="document-heading"><p class="eyebrow">Privacy policy</p><h1>Your browsing<br />stays <em>your business.</em></h1><p>The developer receives no data from Omnibar’s extensions. There are no analytics, telemetry, tracking identifiers, usage reports, or automatic uploads.</p><p class="document-date">Last updated October 4, 2026</p></header>
    <div class="document-body">
      <section><h2>What this policy covers</h2><p>This policy covers Omnibar for Safari and this product website, including the macOS and iPad versions currently being prepared for release. On Mac, one app contains two separately enabled Safari extensions: Omnibar provides the native toolbar palette, and Omnibar Commands provides a centered keyboard palette. On iPad, Omnibar Commands is a keyboard-only Safari web extension that requires a connected hardware keyboard. This policy does not announce availability of development features in an App Store release.</p><p>Omnibar is independent, free, and open source. Contact its developer at <a href={`mailto:${supportEmail}`}>{supportEmail}</a> with privacy questions.</p></section>

      <section><h2>The current address and search text</h2><p>When you open the native Omnibar popover, it asks Safari for the current tab’s URL and page properties needed to identify Private Browsing. It also asks for the originating window’s tab list and available titles and addresses for local tab matching. It holds those details in memory for the popover session and clears that session when the popover closes.</p><p>Omnibar’s extensions do not save a browsing-history database, read the website’s text or forms, fetch search suggestions, or run analytics. The native popover declares website access because Safari requires it before exposing page properties such as the URL.</p><p>When you submit an address, Safari navigates to that destination. When you submit a search, Safari navigates to the selected search provider with your search text in the URL. The destination website or search provider receives that request and may process information under its own privacy policy. Safari may retain navigation history according to its settings and browsing mode.</p></section>

      <section><h2>Tabs and commands</h2><p>Omnibar Commands opens with Command–Shift–P as a centered palette on the current webpage. It has no toolbar palette. On iPad, the shortcut requires a connected hardware keyboard. The palette asks Safari for tab titles, URLs, and state in the window that opened it. It matches tabs locally and holds that information and unsubmitted input in memory for the palette session. It does not save this information, build a search-history index, or send it to the developer.</p><p>Results stay within the originating window and its normal or Private Browsing context. A bundled background script handles the keyboard shortcut and chosen browser actions. A local native-messaging connection handles address resolution and access to shared search settings; it does not contact a server. A request to open settings contains only a fixed action, without search text, tab details, or credentials. You can change settings directly in the Omnibar app on either platform.</p><p>When you invoke the shortcut, temporary access to the current tab allows a small script to display and dismiss the palette. It creates a dialog containing a bundled extension-origin frame, which keeps the palette’s contents and styles separate from the website. It does not read the website’s text, forms, or other page content, and it does not run automatically on every visited page. All interface scripts, styles, and images are bundled locally.</p><p>Safari blocks this display script on its Start Page and other restricted pages. The shortcut opens a local explanation there. Open a normal webpage to use the centered palette. On Mac, you can also use the native Omnibar toolbar button on restricted pages.</p></section>

      <section><h2>Settings stored on your device</h2><p>Omnibar saves search-engine order, removed built-in engine choices, custom engine names and URL templates, and the default website suffix in local preferences. The Omnibar app and its Safari extensions share these preferences through the app’s private local App Group. It uses your device’s Region setting to choose an initial engine order. This does not require location permission or a network lookup.</p><p>The palettes share one local on/off preference for their first-use introduction. Choosing <strong>Got it</strong> dismisses those tips on this device. You can restore them with <strong>Show Palette Tips Again</strong> in the Omnibar setup app. This preference is not synced through Safari or iCloud and contains no search text, addresses, tab information, timestamps, or usage history.</p><p>On Mac, existing settings are imported when you first open the native Omnibar toolbar button after upgrading. This optional import does not overwrite newer choices already saved in the shared settings. Development builds have separate settings and credentials from the production installation.</p><p>These preferences are used to provide the app’s features and are not sent to Omnibar’s developer. You can reorder or remove engines, add removed built-ins back, and edit custom engines in the Omnibar app’s Search Settings. You do not need to enable a Safari extension to configure new settings. <strong>Restore Defaults</strong> restores the built-in engines in the original order for your current region and keeps your custom engines. Custom templates are ordinary preferences; do not put passwords or private session tokens in them.</p></section>

      <section><h2>Optional Kagi private session link</h2><p>If you choose to save a Kagi private session link in its dedicated settings field, Omnibar stores it separately in a local Keychain access group shared by the Omnibar app and its Safari extensions on that device. It is not saved in ordinary preferences. Omnibar does not synchronize the link to iCloud, display it again in settings, or send it to its developer.</p><p>On Mac, to import an existing saved link after upgrading, open Search Settings → Kagi Private Browsing in the original native extension. You can instead save a new link directly in the Omnibar app. Only the original extension reads its previous Keychain item for this import. The old item is retained during migration so an unsuccessful import does not destroy your credential.</p><p>The saved link is used only for Kagi searches when Safari confirms Private Browsing. In that case, the token is included in the navigation request to Kagi, allowing Kagi to identify your session. Direct address navigation and other search engines do not read this credential. The native popover uses the regular Kagi URL if Safari does not supply page properties; Commands uses the originating tab’s Private Browsing state from Safari’s tabs API.</p><p>Removing Kagi from your engine list hides its settings and clears unsaved input. It keeps any saved link in the Keychain, as does <strong>Restore Defaults</strong>. Add Kagi back to make its settings available again.</p><p>Use <strong>Remove Link</strong> in <strong>Search Settings → Kagi Private Browsing</strong> while Kagi is in your engine list to remove the shared credential and prevent it from being imported again. The app cannot delete an older Keychain item retained by the original extension during migration. The original extension deletes that item the next time it checks the private link, or when you use its <strong>Remove Link</strong> action. If Keychain access prevents cleanup, the old item remains until a later successful check; it is not used for searches or imported again. Kagi controls the linked account and session. Its <a href="https://help.kagi.com/kagi/privacy/private-browser-sessions.html">session-link documentation</a> explains how to invalidate a session.</p></section>

      <section><h2>Support messages</h2><p>If you email support, the developer receives your email address and the information you choose to send, and uses that information to respond to your request. Do not send passwords, Kagi session links, or sensitive browsing details.</p></section>

      <section><h2>This website</h2><p>This is a static website hosted on GitHub Pages. The site adds no analytics, tracking pixels, advertising, externally hosted fonts, or client-side tracking scripts.</p><p>GitHub, as the hosting provider, may process technical information about your visit. GitHub states that visitors’ IP addresses are logged for security purposes. See <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/pages/getting-started-with-github-pages/about-github-pages#data-collection">GitHub Pages data collection</a> and <a href="https://docs.github.com/en/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub’s Privacy Statement</a> for details.</p></section>

      <section><h2>Changes and contact</h2><p>If Omnibar’s data handling changes, this policy will be updated and the date above will change. For questions about this policy, contact <a href={`mailto:${supportEmail}`}>{supportEmail}</a>.</p></section>
    </div>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
